WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:05.400
Tom sprints! Tom leaps! Tom&nbsp;
hangs on for dear life. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:08.908
time to set your brain for Cruise control.

00:00:08.997 --> 00:00:13.080
Right out of the gate, we have to&nbsp;
talk about Tom's tendency to run,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:17.684
because this particular trademark has&nbsp;
reached levels of absurdity at this point.

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:18.720
"Don't run."

00:00:19.555 --> 00:00:20.880
"You don't have to chase me."

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:23.650
"You don't have to run."

00:00:23.650 --> 00:00:28.800
Dating all the way back to 1981's "TAPS," Tom&nbsp;
has found a way to show off his incredible knack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:34.740
for sprinting in all but a handful of films.&nbsp;
The legal drama "The Firm" gave him several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:39.780
opportunities to dash around in a suit, and&nbsp;
Cameron Crowe even figured out a way to slip&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:45.120
a sequence of Tom's running technique into&nbsp;
two non-action films: the opening of 2001's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:52.320
mind-bending drama "Vanilla Sky" and the tail end&nbsp;
of the 1996 romantic comedy/drama "Jerry Maguire."

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:59.880
Those were mere warmups. Nowadays, when Tom&nbsp;
runs — he runs. You need only pop in any of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:04.320
the "Mission: Impossible" features to catch&nbsp;
a glimpse of his fancy footwork. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:08.640
J.J. Abrams devotes nearly an entire&nbsp;
minute of the climax of "Mission:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:13.560
Impossible 3" to Tom literally sprinting&nbsp;
in pursuit of the film's key villain.

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:17.940
The latest trailer for "Mission: Impossible&nbsp;
— Dead Reckoning Part One" teases viewers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:23.820
with a brief shot of the man, the myth, the&nbsp;
legend running through a mysterious locale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:30.120
immediately following his name credit. So, yeah,&nbsp;
at this point, it seems Tom is in on the joke.

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:44.220
Tom's wild stunts weren't always a staple in his&nbsp;
films. There are no death-defying leaps off of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:51.360
high rises in "Rain Man" or vertigo-inducing jet&nbsp;
battles in "Eyes Wide Shut," but the actor still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:57.000
managed to show off his need for adrenaline-fueled&nbsp;
antics early in his career. Case in point:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:01.920
In "The Outsiders," Tom's character does a&nbsp;
backflip for no apparent reason after running&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:06.120
out of a house. According to actor Rob Lowe&nbsp;
in an interview with Entertainment Tonight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:10.920
director Francis Ford Coppola asked the&nbsp;
cast to learn how to do a backflip and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:14.580
Tom was the only one capable of&nbsp;
pulling off the difficult feat.

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:15.900
"Check this out, buddy."

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:24.600
The actor also insisted on driving a real&nbsp;
NASCAR racecar in "Days of Thunder," and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:30.540
according to a profile in Rolling Stone, rode&nbsp;
the fastest horse on set during the climactic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:36.060
land race in "Far and Away." It's small potatoes&nbsp;
compared to the stunts he achieves in "The Mummy"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:40.800
and the "Mission: Impossible" films and his&nbsp;
other action-based roles, but worth noting.

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:44.940
So, why does he do it? During an&nbsp;
appearance on "The Graham Norton&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:48.900
Show," Tom admitted it all comes&nbsp;
down to the storytelling, saying:

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:51.480
"I feel that you're bringing everything, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:57.120
physically and emotionally, to a character&nbsp;
in a story. I've trained for 30 years doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:02.426
[stunts] and it allows us to put cameras in&nbsp;
places where you are normally not able to."

00:03:02.636 --> 00:03:09.600
Not only is Tom Cruise a thrill junkie, but&nbsp;
he also loves himself a slick motorcycle — a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:17.160
fact made abundantly clear when the actor&nbsp;
memorably rode a Kawasaki GPZ900R along an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:22.680
airport runway in 1986's "Top Gun."&nbsp;
Over 35 years later, Tom once again&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:29.340
hopped aboard a Kawasaki — the Ninja H2 to&nbsp;
be exact, a.k.a. the fastest motorcycle in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:34.200
the world — for the enormously successful&nbsp;
sequel "Top Gun: Maverick." In the interim,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:38.520
Tom has thrilled audiences with his astonishing&nbsp;
motorcycle skills in John Woo's "Mission:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:42.960
Impossible II," Joseph Kosinski's "Oblivion,"&nbsp;
and "Mission: Impossible — Fallout."

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:47.640
Tom's love for motorcycles extends off-screen&nbsp;
as well. Apparently, after wrapping a project,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:52.320
the actor will take home or purchase&nbsp;
the motorcycle used in the film and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:57.720
add it to his vast collection. According&nbsp;
to Motorious, Tom has in his possession&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:04.560
the BMW S1000 RR used in "Mission: Impossible&nbsp;
— Rogue Nation," the Triumph Thruxton used in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:09.300
"Edge of Tomorrow," and yes, even that&nbsp;
Kawasaki from "Top Gun," among others.

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:14.880
And if motorcycles don't exist in the context&nbsp;
of the movie, Tom will usually hop on a horse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:21.568
instead, but we say that counts. What is&nbsp;
a horse if not a motorcycle with legs?

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:26.940
While many of Tom's movies feature the box&nbsp;
office star working alongside a group of notable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:33.300
co-stars, there always seems to be a moment where&nbsp;
his character must cut the rip cord and go solo to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:37.920
save the day. We see this often in the "Mission:&nbsp;
Impossible" franchise in which Tom's IMF Agent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:43.680
Ethan Hunt leaves his teammates in the dust to&nbsp;
go one-on-one with the film's principal bad guy.

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:50.280
"We didn't have this conversation. You don't&nbsp;
know where I am, if I'm dead or alive."

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:55.860
But this trait also extends to some of the&nbsp;
actor's more dramatic ventures. Notably,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.860 --> 00:05:01.140
in "A Few Good Men," Tom's cocky lawyer&nbsp;
ignores the advice of his team and takes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.140 --> 00:05:04.860
on Jack Nicholson's sharp-tongued&nbsp;
Colonel Nathan Jessep all on his own.

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:09.660
Likewise, in 2008's "Valkyrie," based&nbsp;
on real events, Tom's one-armed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:14.400
one-eyed Colonel plots with an assortment&nbsp;
of German officers to assassinate Adolf&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:18.780
Hitler and ends up planting the bomb&nbsp;
himself. Other Cruise vehicles such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:23.105
as "Minority Report," "Jack Reacher,"&nbsp;
"Born on the Fourth of July," and "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.105 --> 00:05:29.029
Firm" position Tom against an evil or corrupt&nbsp;
establishment that he must battle on his own.

00:05:29.029 --> 00:05:32.804
"I am not a hero. I'm a&nbsp;
drifter with nothing to lose."

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:39.120
You can't really discuss Tom Cruise without&nbsp;
acknowledging his vast collection of shades.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:43.380
Whether he's mixing drinks on the beach,&nbsp;
driving Dustin Hoffman cross country,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.380 --> 00:05:49.860
or simply jumping out of explosions, Tom&nbsp;
Cruise has an eye for a classy pair of eyewear.

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:55.320
In "Risky Business" and "Top Gun," Tom&nbsp;
slid on Ray-Bans and helped popularize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.320 --> 00:05:58.312
the brand in the 1980s, according to Mental Floss.

00:05:58.620 --> 00:06:05.340
"There's one thing I've learned in all my&nbsp;
years. Sometimes you gotta say, 'What the f—.'"

00:06:05.340 --> 00:06:09.480
And in "Top Gun: Maverick," the&nbsp;
shades were so important that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.480 --> 00:06:12.720
his Ray-Bans were custom-made to fit his face.

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:19.020
In other words: It ain't a Tom Cruise film&nbsp;
unless it features Tom Cruise in sunglasses.

00:06:26.106 --> 00:06:26.986
"Thank you."

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:33.960
Aside from a few exceptions, Tom is typically the&nbsp;
de facto star of the films he appears in. So yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:38.940
it makes sense that his character always ends up&nbsp;
saving the day from the forces of evil. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.940 --> 00:06:44.280
that's outside of films where he is the force&nbsp;
of evil, like "Interview with the Vampire" and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:48.840
"Collateral." And even in "Collateral," his&nbsp;
cold-hearted assassin influences Jamie Foxx's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:53.160
world-weary cab driver enough to force a&nbsp;
positive change in the young man's life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:58.080
which can technically be seen as&nbsp;
a win for Tom. Without Vincent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:04.440
it's doubtful Max ever finds the confidence needed&nbsp;
to dump his crappy job and move on with his life.

00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:09.480
Otherwise, Tom is typically the man of&nbsp;
the hour by the final reel of any film.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:14.760
Whether its stopping dastardly terrorists or&nbsp;
corrupt operatives from destroying the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.760 --> 00:07:20.700
saving Iceman and Rooster in the "Top Gun"&nbsp;
films, or taking down an alien tripod to save his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:25.020
daughter in "War of the Worlds," you can pretty&nbsp;
much count on Tom Cruise to get the job done.

00:07:30.721 --> 00:07:31.679
"Yeah baby."

00:07:32.477 --> 00:07:38.880
No matter the circumstances, Tom Cruise characters&nbsp;
always get the girl. In "Risky Business," he ends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:44.280
up with Lana, the sensitive sex worker. "Top&nbsp;
Gun" sees our beleaguered hero ride off with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.280 --> 00:07:50.520
Kelly McGillis, and "Minority Report" rewards his&nbsp;
efforts by fixing him up with his estranged wife.

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:54.960
In "A Few Good Men," it's strongly&nbsp;
hinted that Demi Moore has eyes for Tom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:59.220
while Cameron Diaz locks lips with&nbsp;
our main man in "Knight and Day."

00:07:59.220 --> 00:08:06.540
Those are just a few examples, but it's clear:&nbsp;
Tom's characters can get any woman they want. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.540 --> 00:08:10.740
whether they're attracted to the killer&nbsp;
good looks, impeccable fashion sense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.740 --> 00:08:14.460
or heart swelling one-liners&nbsp;
is a topic worth discussing.

00:08:15.147 --> 00:08:20.820
Tom Cruise may be the king of cool, but&nbsp;
even the most even-keeled action heroes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.820 --> 00:08:26.820
lose their patience every now and again. And&nbsp;
God forbid you get in his way when it happens.

00:08:29.940 --> 00:08:34.260
As it turns out, an explosive temper is&nbsp;
practically a trademark of his characters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.260 --> 00:08:40.080
It's little wonder that Ben Stiller cast&nbsp;
him as the foul-mouthed Les Grossman in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:44.280
the 2008 comedy "Tropic Thunder."&nbsp;
Hidden under layers of prosthetics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.280 --> 00:08:47.820
Tom shouts, curses, and dances away with the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.820 --> 00:08:53.460
revealing a darker side that somehow fits&nbsp;
snugly amidst the comic antics of the movie.

00:08:53.460 --> 00:08:58.380
More dramatically, in "A Few Good Men," Tom&nbsp;
rages against Demi Moore after she makes an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.380 --> 00:09:03.420
error and even does the "toss everything off&nbsp;
the desk" bit for good measure. In "Mission:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.420 --> 00:09:09.000
Impossible," he justifiably loses his cool&nbsp;
after his entire team is killed during a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:13.500
mission gone wrong. He even gets a little&nbsp;
grumpy at his kids in "War of the Worlds."

00:09:13.907 --> 00:09:15.167
"I'm not hungry either."

00:09:15.421 --> 00:09:16.450
"You're not hungry either?

00:09:17.305 --> 00:09:18.432
Fine." 

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:27.000
These are but a few examples of&nbsp;
Tom's ability to blow a gasket,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:34.027
but make no mistake, Tom has many captivating&nbsp;
tantrums throughout his filmography.

00:09:34.215 --> 00:09:40.320
Save for his overweight character Les Grossman,&nbsp;
Tom typically carries an incredible physique&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.320 --> 00:09:45.060
that he's clearly not afraid to show&nbsp;
off at every possible opportunity.

00:09:46.200 --> 00:09:53.700
In "Jack Reacher," for example, Tom sheds&nbsp;
his shirt and attempts to carry a serious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.700 --> 00:09:59.040
conversation with Rosamund Pike's Helen Rodin.&nbsp;
Likewise, in the action comedy "Knight and Day,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.040 --> 00:10:05.100
Tom wows Cameron Diaz during a foray on a tropical&nbsp;
island. In "Mission: Impossible — Rogue Nation,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.100 --> 00:10:10.740
he sheds his top for an exciting fight sequence&nbsp;
during which he crunches up a pole to escape. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.740 --> 00:10:17.160
who could forget the infamous volleyball scene&nbsp;
in "Top Gun?" Hey, if you've got it, flaunt it.

00:10:17.160 --> 00:10:19.080
"I'm sorry, could you please put a shirt on?"

00:10:19.860 --> 00:10:20.760
"This is my shirt."

00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:26.460
We've already discussed Tom's obsession&nbsp;
with motorcycles, but that's only part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.460 --> 00:10:31.500
the story. Nearly every Tom Cruise movie&nbsp;
features the actor going really fast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.500 --> 00:10:35.880
either by running, driving, flying,&nbsp;
boating, or vampiric trickery.

00:10:41.340 --> 00:10:46.140
"Top Gun" is the obvious choice here, but&nbsp;
also consider Ron Howard's dramatic epic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.140 --> 00:10:51.360
"Far and Away," in which Tom hops on a horse&nbsp;
and outruns everyone to claim his piece of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:56.460
Oklahoma property. Or, how about "Minority&nbsp;
Report," where he leaps around on cars on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.460 --> 00:11:01.500
futuristic highway? He rides a speeding&nbsp;
car off a bridge in "Vanilla Sky," and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.500 --> 00:11:05.760
of course, much of "Days of Thunder"&nbsp;
takes place on a race track.

00:11:05.760 --> 00:11:08.880
So, yeah, Tom Cruise means it when he says…

00:11:08.880 --> 00:11:13.440
"I feel the need, the need for speed."

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:14.400
"Ow!"

00:11:14.400 --> 00:11:21.060
And while almost all his movies feature Tom's&nbsp;
characters going fast, it's not uncommon for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.060 --> 00:11:25.740
actor himself to be hitting the same speeds.&nbsp;
In fact, Newsweek notes that in "Top Gun:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.740 --> 00:11:30.060
Maverick," the actor took to the skies&nbsp;
in an actual fighter jet, while "Mission:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.060 --> 00:11:34.560
Impossible — Fallout" gave him a chance to fly&nbsp;
a helicopter through snow-covered mountains.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.340 --> 00:11:40.440
Smithsonian Magazine details how "American Made"&nbsp;
allowed the actor to put his pilot's license to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.440 --> 00:11:47.700
use and perform intense flying sequences in a&nbsp;
six-seater Piper Aerostar 600 and a Cessna 414.

00:11:53.520 --> 00:11:59.520
It's a weird trademark, but at some point in many&nbsp;
of Tom's films, the actor is forced to hang onto&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.520 --> 00:12:04.440
something for dear life. "Mission: Impossible —&nbsp;
Rogue Nation," might take the cake for literal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.440 --> 00:12:10.320
representation of it, but there are actually&nbsp;
so many instances of the superstar clinging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:14.880
to the side of something or other in a desperate&nbsp;
attempt to keep himself from falling. In "Knight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.880 --> 00:12:20.280
and Day," the action maestro hangs onto Cameron&nbsp;
Diaz's car while battling bad guys; in "War of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.280 --> 00:12:25.380
the Worlds" he hangs onto a group of strangers&nbsp;
to avoid being sucked into an alien ship; and he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.380 --> 00:12:31.260
spends most of "Rock of Ages" barely clinging&nbsp;
to his own sanity. Which…that counts, right?

00:12:38.280 --> 00:12:46.800
Perhaps most famously, Tom hung off of the side&nbsp;
of the massive Burj Khalifa building for "Mission&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.800 --> 00:12:51.300
Impossible: Ghost Protocol." Even "Edge of&nbsp;
Tomorrow" featured the actor clinging to an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.300 --> 00:12:56.700
object — a machine gun attached to a military&nbsp;
aircraft — during the action-packed climax.

00:12:56.700 --> 00:12:59.760
At the end of the day, we'll continue to enjoy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.760 --> 00:13:04.860
his many death-defying feats for as&nbsp;
long as he's willing to perform them.

